 ## Overview
-Greedy Meshing (Back Face) is a specialized surface extraction method that generates quads for the back-facing surfaces (negative Z direction) of a voxel chunk [S1, unofficial]. It extends the 2D greedy meshing algorithm to three dimensions by extracting 2D slices from the 3D voxel grid along the Z-axis and processing them to merge adjacent blocks of the same type into larger rectangles [S1, unofficial]. It is distinct from general greedy meshing [3519-greedy-meshing.md] because it operates exclusively on the back-facing boundary, requiring specific handling of neighbor chunks to determine visibility at the chunk's rear edge.
